$\begin{align}
\sin(y)&=-5x
\\\\
\cos(y)\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dt}&=-5\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$

We are given that $\dfrac{dy}{dt}=10$, and we want to find $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ when $y=-\pi$.

Let's plug $\blueD{y=-\pi}$ and $\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}=10}$ into the equation we obtained:

$\begin{align}
\cos(\blueD{y})\cdot\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}}&=-5\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
\cos(\blueD{-\pi})\cdot\goldD{10}&=-5\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
-10&=-5\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
2&=\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$
